From 7ee51249729bd5107de49777c0cfb75da2413c8f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shaurizard <contact@nyoom.engineering>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:40:44 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 47/48] feat(audio): follow the save-file music volume of PGR3,
 PGR4 and DOAX2

Some titles keep their music volume in their own save data and never send
XMPSetVolume, so the XMP player stays at its default volume whatever the
player picks in the options menu. PGR4 is one: it never sends XMPSetVolume
(0x7000C), and Canary's table puts its music slider (0-10) at byte 2 of
the 344-byte savegame record.

This ports Xenia Canary's XmpVolumePatch (451f915,
src/xenia/kernel/util/xmp_volume_patch.{h,cc} and its hooks in
kernel_state.cc, xboxkrnl_io.cc and xam_input.cc). For a known title it
watches reads and writes of the save file, maps the volume byte to
[0.0001, 1] and hands it to the player; after a read it also keeps the
guest address of the record and re-reads the byte whenever a controller
packet number changes, so the slider takes effect before the game saves.
Titles: DOAX2 (544307D2, ups.dat), PGR3 (4D5307D1, profile.save) and PGR4
(4D5307F9, savegame).

Differences from Canary:

- The record address, last volume and last packet number are atomics;
  file I/O and input polling come from different guest threads.
- The polled byte is read only while its page is still readable, and the
  address is dropped once it is not, since the record buffer belongs to
  the title.
- KernelState creates the patch in SetExecutableModule as Canary does but
  does not free it in TerminateTitle: guest threads that ignore the
  cooperative exit keep running there and may still be inside a hook.
  The next SetExecutableModule replaces it.
- The read hook runs on the status of the read itself.

Co-authored-by: factory-droid[bot] <138933559+factory-droid[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>
